/*
RMSTEST_33b - is create_object actually locked to <OBJECTS_GENERATION>
CLAUDE.md tracked debt: validate()'s RMS0304 wrong-section check

Companion to 33a, and the pair is the point - see 33a's header for why RMS0304
is unbuilt and what a per-command answer is for. Run them in the same sitting.

QUESTION
Same question, opposite direction: create_object placed in
<TERRAIN_GENERATION>. Two commands answering DIFFERENTLY would be the most
useful result this pair can produce, because it would kill any hope of a single
blanket rule and force RMS0304 to be a data-driven per-command list - which is
also the only form of the check that fits this project's "vocabulary is
data-driven" rule.

METHOD
A minimal map with an empty <OBJECTS_GENERATION> and one create_object sitting
in <TERRAIN_GENERATION>. The object histogram is the binary reading.

GOLD is chosen over a unit because it is gaia, needs no player, and appears in
the export with an unambiguous count. set_gaia_object_only makes that explicit
rather than relying on a default.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. 40 GOLD objects appear, matching 33a's prediction 1, and section headers are
   organisational for both. This is the outcome that most constrains us: RMS0304
   would be a style hint at best, and the honest thing would be not to build it.
2. No GOLD. Combined with 33a, either both are locked (build the check as a
   warning, driven by language.json's section field) or exactly one is (build it
   from a measured per-command list, and note the field alone is insufficient).
3. GOLD appears but at the wrong count, or in the wrong place. Record the
   discrepancy - it would mean the command ran with a different stage's
   constraints applied, which is a third category neither 33a nor this script
   was designed for and which nothing in Sec.9 currently models.

WATCH FOR
Objects placed in a terrain stage may be positioned before terrain exists, so a
count of 40 with all of them clustered oddly is prediction 3, not prediction 1.
Check positions, not just the count. This is the same mistake shape as reading a
grown quantity when a counted one was available.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  Default object histogram gives the count.
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --clusters GOLD
  gives the positions, which is what separates prediction 1 from prediction 3.

ONE RUN, unless prediction 3 fires.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* DELIBERATELY IN THE WRONG SECTION. This is the test. */
  create_object GOLD
  {
    number_of_objects 40
    set_gaia_object_only
    temp_min_distance_group_placement 6
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
